<p><b>Patricia Thomas, 15 Septembre 2001, 10h00</b></p>
<p>Bonjour Michel et Guy,</p>
<p>You will excuse me if I write in my mother tongue, but at this particular time 
  I feel very American, notwithstanding the fact that I have now lived a few more 
  years outside the States than within. Canada, and more precisely, Quebec is 
  my adopted home. But somehow this week I have found that above all, no matter 
  how many countries I live and travel in or how many other passports I may have, 
  I am and always will be an American, no matter. <img src="flag.jpg" width="302" height="400" align="left">This 
  week I even felt the need to let it be known...it's not that I usually hide 
  it, but I have felt enough at home and among kindred spirits in Quebec, that 
  I have never felt the need to. And I will say that conversations about the US 
  and about Americans with Canadians are hardly ever pleasant here. In lieu of 
  accepting or combating the constant criticism of the US that is inevitable in 
  discussions, at parties, on planes..(much of which I am in agreement) , I have 
  usually chosen to say nothing, to blend in as another Canadian, or Quebecois 
  (my accent always gives me away here). This week, however I wanted to show that 
  I was American. Strangely enough I found myself wanting to find an American 
  flag to put outside my home in Quebec. I searched and searched and came up with 
  a small flag that I had had with me in Africa in the late seventies when I worked 
  as a Peace Corps volunteer. I put it in the middle of the dining room table. 
  I lit candles and attended a memorial service at the cathedral. I took my children, 
  and told them that they too were Americans.</p>
<p>I feel that this is a time to grieve, to deplore the actions of the terrorists 
  who would take away everyone's freedom. It is a time not to criticize the past 
  , but to contribute to the construction of a new world....to contribute what 
  Canada and Quebec may have to offer in constructing the new world of which they 
  are a part. In crises, there is pain and possibility...possibility to build 
  something better...together.</p>
<p>Michel, as an American who feels strongly about the week's events, thank you 
  for your response.</p>
<p>Patricia Thomas</p>
<p></p>
